---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"

export const prerender = true

// These sections are built only after the owner lists the corresponding capability and redeploys.
const listed = new Set((process.env.CODIUM_LISTED_PERKS ?? "").split(",").map((value) => value.trim()))
const githubCodium = listed.has("github-agents")
const githubSchedules = githubCodium && listed.has("scheduled")
const githubNoSecret = githubCodium && listed.has("github-no-secret")
---

<DocsPage id="vectorscope">

          <p class="dx-lede">
            Vectorscope is Vector's code review bot. It reads the repository around a change, not just the diff, and says
            what is wrong with it — on a pull request, on your branch before you push, or on the work sitting in your
            editor right now.
          </p>

          <h2 id="where-it-runs">Where Vectorscope runs</h2>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>On every pull request</dt><dd>Say yes to automatic review during <code>vector github install</code>. Vectorscope reviews each pull request when it opens and again on every push, and answers <code>/vectorscope</code> in a comment.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Before you push</dt><dd><code>vector vectorscope</code> in your terminal runs the same review on your branch against its merge base, or on your uncommitted work with <code>--uncommitted</code>. Nothing is posted anywhere.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>In the workspace</dt><dd>The desktop app's Pull Requests panel reviews a pull request in place and lets you post the result, or keep it to yourself.</dd></div>
          </dl>

          <h2 id="pull-requests">On a pull request</h2>
          <p>
            Vectorscope leaves one summary comment with the risk, what changed and the files that matter, and comments
            on the exact lines, each with a severity and, where it is safe, a fix you can commit from GitHub. It reads
            the repository, not just the diff: it opens the changed files, follows their callers and checks the tests.
            On the next push it reviews only what changed and says what got fixed instead of repeating itself.
          </p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>What you get</dt><dd>A summary that is edited in place, and one review per push that contains only new comments. Severities are Blocking, Concern and Nit. Nits and style points are left out unless you set <code>nits</code> to true; anything outside the changed lines stays in the summary. Vectorscope only ever comments. It never approves or blocks a pull request, unless you set <code>failOn</code> to make its check fail on blocking issues.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Commands</dt><dd><code>/vectorscope review</code> reviews again, <code>/vectorscope review full</code> starts over, and <code>/vectorscope pause</code> or <code>/vectorscope resume</code> turns automatic reviews off or on for one pull request (or add the <code>vector:paused</code> label). Reply <code>/vectorscope fix</code> on a comment and Vectorscope applies that fix. <code>/vs</code> is the short form; <code>/vector</code> and <code>/vx</code> still work, so workflows installed before the name existed keep running. General coding tasks on issues continue to use <code>/vector</code> or <code>/vx</code>. A person with write access, or the pull request's author, can resolve a comment or react to it with a thumbs-down, and Vectorscope won't raise it again. Commands need write access.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Your rules</dt><dd>Write plain-English rules in <code>.vector/review.md</code>. A <code>## path: src/billing/**</code> heading scopes the rules under it. Tune limits in <code>.vector/review.json</code>: <code>maxComments</code> (10), <code>minSeverity</code> (concern), <code>minConfidence</code> (0.7), <code>nits</code> (false), <code>ignore</code>, <code>security</code>, <code>maxCostUsd</code>, <code>triageModel</code>. Both are read from the base branch, so a pull request can't change the rules it is reviewed under, except by editing the workflow file, which needs write access.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Low noise</dt><dd>Lockfiles, build output, generated and vendored files, snapshots and binaries are skipped and listed. Drafts, dependency bots and pull requests labelled <code>vector:skip</code> are skipped. To review drafts too, delete the <code>draft == false</code> line from <code>.github/workflows/vector.yml</code>. Very large pull requests get a note with a cost estimate instead of a review.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Cost</dt><dd>Your model provider bills usage under your own credentials. Every review ends with the model, the tokens, what it cost and the total for that pull request. Each review stops at <code>maxCostUsd</code> ($2.00), automatic reviews of one pull request stop at <code>maxCostUsdPerPr</code> ($10.00), and <code>REVIEW_MAX_COST_USD_PER_MONTH</code> in the workflow caps a month. Set <code>REVIEW_AUTO_MODEL</code> to use a cheaper model for automatic reviews, or <code>triageModel</code> in <code>.vector/review.json</code> to run the first pass on a cheaper model and double-check its findings on your review model.</dd></div>
          </dl>

          <h2 id="choose-a-model">Choose a model</h2>
          <p class="note">After upgrading to 1.99.99, repositories using Vector's GitHub workflow should run <code>vector github install</code> again. The regenerated workflow uses <code>VECTOR_*</code> configuration variables.</p>
          <p>
            With your own key selected, reviews and GitHub runs use your provider credentials. The <code>VECTOR_CLI_TOKEN</code>
            secret authenticates your Vector account; it does not provide model access. Add the provider key as a
            repository secret and pass it into each job that calls Vector, using the provider's environment variable,
            such as <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> or <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>. Set <code>MODEL</code> in the job's
            environment to a model that provider offers, in <code>provider/model</code> format.
          </p>
          <p>
            New workflows connect the selected provider's secret automatically. Check older workflows too: provider-key
            lines that are commented out do not pass the secret into the run. If no model is configured or available,
            Vector stops with setup instructions instead of selecting an unavailable model. Set
            <code>REVIEW_AUTO_MODEL</code> to choose a different model for automatic reviews, or set <code>model</code>
            in <code>.vector/review.json</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a local review, connect a provider with <code>vector auth login</code> and choose a model with
            <code>--model provider/model</code>, your review configuration, or Vector's configured default.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            From 1.99.99, the former third-party hosted model providers are no longer available, even with your own
            API key for them. Connect another provider for reviews and GitHub runs.
          </p>

          <h2 id="github">Task in, pull request out</h2>
          <p>
            Vector works in your own repo, on your own Actions minutes. Comment
            <code>/vector fix the flaky auth test</code> on an issue to ask for a branch and pull request, or comment
            on an existing pull request to continue its work. Only people with current write access can start a task.
            Tasks are limited to that repository's software: its code, tests, docs, builds, reviews and maintenance.
          </p>
          <p>
            GitHub runs the workflow and bills its Actions minutes under your account and GitHub's terms. Check your
            repository's Actions settings and allowance before starting. Your model credentials or Codium plan pay for
            model access; they do not pay GitHub's runner bill.
          </p>

          {githubCodium && (
            <>
              <h3 id="github-codium">Use Codium</h3>
              <p>
                Run <code>vector github install</code> in the repository and select Codium. Install the Vector GitHub
                App for the repository, merge the generated workflow into its default branch, and follow setup's
                one-time binding instructions. The binding connects this repository to your Vector account.
                {githubNoSecret && <> Codium model access uses your plan, with no provider API key or model secret to add.</>}
              </p>
              <p>
                People with write access to a bound repository use the binder's plan when they start a run. Review that
                access before binding a shared repository. The binder can remove the connection from Settings → GitHub
                or the GitHub section of their account page. Codium runs require GitHub's runners; runners you manage
                are refused.
              </p>
            </>
          )}

          <h3 id="github-own-key">Use your own key</h3>
          <p>
            Run <code>vector github install --own-key</code>, choose a provider and model, and merge the generated
            <code>.github/workflows/vector.yml</code> into the default branch. Add <code>VECTOR_CLI_TOKEN</code> from
            your free Vector account and the selected provider's key as repository secrets. The CLI explains the exact
            secret names. Your provider bills model usage under your credentials. This path needs no Codium plan.
          </p>
          <p>
            The account token identifies your Vector account; it does not supply model access. Conversation sharing
            needs your explicit choice. With your own key on a runner you manage, Vector cannot provide the process
            isolation used on supported GitHub runners. Local Vectorscope review remains available without connecting
            GitHub: use <code>vector vectorscope</code> with your configured provider.
          </p>

          <h3 id="github-limits">Agent time and limits</h3>
          <p>
            Agent time is how long a run is active, from its start to its last activity, capped at the plan's longest
            run. Vector measures it; it is not GitHub minutes. See <a href="/docs/codium#plans">Codium plans</a> for the
            current allowance, simultaneous runs and longest run. A stopped run keeps completed work and gives a plain
            reason; it does not claim the remaining files were reviewed.
          </p>

          <h3 id="github-schedules">Scheduled repository work</h3>
          <p>
            With your own key, configure repository schedules during setup or in the generated workflow. Each schedule
            has a stable id and task. A repeat updates that schedule's own bot-created proposal only while its head is
            unchanged; human edits are preserved. GitHub cron runs on UTC time and is best effort: runs may be delayed
            or missed, daylight-saving changes can shift local time, and GitHub disables schedules after 60 days of
            inactivity in a public repository.
          </p>
          {githubSchedules && (
            <p>
              Codium schedules also use the binder's plan and require its scheduled-work allowance. Setup refuses a
              schedule that the current plan does not allow. A schedule's agent time and GitHub minutes are separate.
            </p>
          )}

          <h3 id="github-run-in-github">Start from Vector</h3>
          <p>
            In the desktop app, choose Run in GitHub for a repository with a merged Vector workflow. Sign in to GitHub
            to select the repository and start its workflow. GitHub keeps the run, branch and pull request available
            after your computer disconnects. This still runs in your own repo, on your own Actions minutes.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            Under Settings → Actions → General → Workflow permissions, enable “Allow GitHub Actions to create and
            approve pull requests” if you want Vector to open a pull request. Otherwise it can provide a compare link.
            Pull requests created with the default <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> do not start your other CI workflows.
            Explicitly opting in to Vector App publishing lets eligible tasks start that CI, which can spend additional
            Actions minutes. Inline review replies and tasks containing non-writer content retain the repository-token
            publication path.
          </p>

          <h3 id="github-follow-ups">Continue the conversation</h3>
          <p>
            Reply on the issue or pull request with another <code>/vector</code> task to continue. The result links to
            its workflow run and includes the changes and checks the agent actually completed, with exit codes. If the
            repository or branch has moved, Vector checks the current head before publishing and preserves human work.
          </p>

          <h3 id="github-privacy">Repository data and logs</h3>
          <p>
            GitHub Actions logs are public on public repositories. Do not put credentials or private material in a task,
            comment or command output. The workflow keeps conversation state in the repository's private Actions
            artifacts when available; GitHub controls artifact access and retention. Treat downloaded artifacts as
            repository data. With your own key, public conversation sharing requires separate explicit approval.
          </p>
          <p>
            On supported GitHub runners, Vector withholds credentials from the commands and servers it starts. This
            includes a step's own secret-looking environment variables and tokens configured for MCP servers, formatters
            or language servers; a command that needs one may fail. A missing or unreadable conversation artifact starts
            from the GitHub thread instead of blocking the task.
          </p>

          <h2 id="before-you-push">Before you push</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector vectorscope</code> (or <code>vector review</code>) runs the same review on your branch against
            its merge-base with the default branch. Use <code>--uncommitted</code> for work in progress,
            <code>--json</code> for scripts, and <code>--fail-on blocking</code> in a pre-push hook.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector vectorscope
vector vectorscope --uncommitted
vector vectorscope --pr 482 --json</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>

          <h2 id="cli-github">GitHub from the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector pr &lt;number&gt;</code> fetches a pull request's branch, checks it out and starts Vector in that working tree — the shortcut for picking a review conversation up on your own machine.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector pr 482
vector github review --pr 482 --dry-run</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            The generated workflow calls two commands you can also run yourself. <code>vector github run</code> executes the agent for a GitHub event, with <code>--event</code> for a payload and <code>--token</code> for a personal access token. <code>vector github review</code> reviews a pull request and posts the result; <code>--dry-run</code> prints every write it would make as JSON instead of posting, which is how you test a configuration change without commenting on a real pull request.
          </p>

          <h2 id="review-pipeline">How a review runs</h2>
          <p>
            Vectorscope is one engine behind three doors: the GitHub Action, <code>vector vectorscope</code> in your
            terminal, and the Pull Requests panel in the desktop app. They share the same prompts, the same output
            schema, the same filters and the same summary. This is what happens between a trigger and a comment.
          </p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li>
              <span />
              <div>
                <strong>A trigger arrives</strong>
                <p>
                  A pull request opens or takes a push, or someone comments a command. Vectorscope answers to
                  <code>/vectorscope</code>, <code>/vs</code>, <code>/vector</code> and <code>/vx</code>, longest name
                  first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span />
              <div>
                <strong>The skip ladder decides whether to run</strong>
                <p>Ten rules in order, first match wins. Most of them only apply to automatic runs.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span />
              <div>
                <strong>The run is planned</strong>
                <p>
                  Which specialists run, how much diff fits the model's context, and what the run is allowed to spend.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span />
              <div>
                <strong>Each specialist reads the code</strong>
                <p>
                  A read-only session per specialist, with the changed files, their callers, their history and your
                  rules — not just the diff.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span />
              <div>
                <strong>A verify pass checks the candidates</strong>
                <p>A second pass re-reads the code at each cited location and confirms or rejects the finding.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span />
              <div>
                <strong>Selection decides what is worth saying</strong>
                <p>Confidence, severity and caps decide what goes on a line, what goes in the summary, and what is dropped.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span />
              <div>
                <strong>The review is posted</strong>
                <p>One summary comment, edited in place, and only the comments that are new since the last push.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h3 id="reading-the-command">Reading the command</h3>
          <p>
            The parser strips HTML comments, code fences and quoted lines before it looks for anything, so showing or
            quoting a command never runs it. Only the first line that starts with a mention is read. A known verb runs
            that verb; anything else is treated as a task for the agent rather than ignored.
          </p>

          <h3 id="skip-ladder">The skip ladder</h3>
          <p>Rules apply in this order and the first match wins:</p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>month-budget</strong><span>The monthly cap is spent. This one has no command exception.</span></li>
            <li><strong>label</strong><span>A <code>vector:paused</code>, <code>vector:skip</code> or <code>no-review</code> label. Automatic runs only.</span></li>
            <li><strong>draft</strong><span>Draft pull requests. Automatic runs only.</span></li>
            <li><strong>author</strong><span>Dependabot and Renovate by default. Automatic runs only.</span></li>
            <li><strong>branch</strong><span>Branches matching a glob you set. Automatic runs only.</span></li>
            <li><strong>fork</strong><span>Automatic runs only — a command on a fork <em>does</em> run, in untrusted mode.</span></li>
            <li><strong>already-reviewed</strong><span>This head is already reviewed. Not overridable by <code>/vectorscope review</code>; only <code>review full</code> clears it.</span></li>
            <li><strong>pr-budget</strong><span>This pull request has spent its dollar cap. Automatic runs only.</span></li>
            <li><strong>nothing-to-review</strong><span>Every changed file was ignored — lockfiles, generated, vendored, binary.</span></li>
            <li><strong>too-large</strong><span>More than 300 files or 5,000 changed lines. Automatic runs only; the summary carries a cost estimate instead.</span></li>
          </ul>

          <h3 id="planning">Planning the run</h3>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div>
              <dt>Which specialists</dt>
              <dd>
                The code reviewer always runs. A second, security-only reviewer joins it when <code>security</code> is
                <code>auto</code> (the default) and the change touches a sensitive path — authentication, crypto, SQL,
                payments, CI workflows, Dockerfiles — or whenever you set <code>always</code>. Each reports only its own
                kind of defect, so one problem is not posted twice.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>How much diff</dt>
              <dd>
                The budget is taken from the model's context: about 30% of what is left after a reserve, which is
                roughly 16,800 characters at 32k of context and 79,800 at 128k, capped by <code>maxDiffChars</code>
                (120,000). Files that do not fit inline are still reachable with the read tool.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Which models</dt>
              <dd>
                Any model with at least 32k tokens of context. Below that the review refuses rather than truncating,
                and says which model and what size it has.
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>

          <h3 id="what-the-reviewer-sees">What the reviewer sees</h3>
          <p>
            A review is not a diff in a prompt. Each specialist gets a read-only session and, with it:
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>The changed files at head</strong><span>The diff, and the reviewer opens the changed files in full. On an untrusted pull request the working tree is the base branch, so the prompt carries each changed file's head version, once: a numbered view with the added and removed lines marked replaces its diff where that is no longer than both together. A file over 200 lines keeps 20 lines around each change and folds the rest; a file too large to include is shown by its hunks with context. A re-review shows files with nothing new by their changed lines only.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Their callers</strong><span>Declarations found in the added lines, then grep hits for where those symbols are used elsewhere.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Their history</strong><span><code>git log</code> and blame for the changed ranges, so a line that was deliberately written that way reads as such.</span></li>
            <li><strong>The conversation</strong><span>Human review comments already on the pull request.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Your rules</strong><span>Plain English from <code>.vector/review.md</code>, scoped by <code>## path:</code> headings, read from the base branch.</span></li>
            <li><strong>What is still open</strong><span>Findings from earlier runs, and what your team has dismissed elsewhere in the repository.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">
            The tools are <code>read</code>, <code>grep</code>, <code>glob</code> and <code>list</code>. The reviewer
            cannot edit files, run commands or browse the web. Vector's own code gathers the git history and posts the
            result.
          </p>

          <h3 id="verify-pass">The verify pass</h3>
          <p>
            Candidate findings go to a second pass that re-reads the code at each cited location and answers
            <strong>confirmed</strong> only when the defect is real and triggers as described, otherwise
            <strong>rejected</strong> with a one-sentence reason. Each candidate is handed over as untrusted text — a
            claim to check, never an instruction — because the first pass wrote it after reading code the pull
            request's author controls. On an untrusted pull request a committable fix is offered only after the verify
            pass confirms the finding.
          </p>
          <p>
            With <code>triageModel</code> set in <code>.vector/review.json</code>, the review and security passes run on
            that model, and every finding above a nit is then checked on your review model, even with
            <code>verify</code> set to off. Only the findings your review model confirmed are posted. An earlier
            finding the cheaper pass calls fixed is marked fixed, and its thread resolved, only when your review model
            confirms that too. If that check doesn't finish, the cheaper pass's findings are left out, earlier findings
            stay open, and the summary says so.
            Nits, when you turn them on, are listed in the summary without that check. <code>vector review</code> and
            the desktop panel list what each pass spent when a review has more than one pass.
          </p>

          <h3 id="second-lab">Checked by a second lab</h3>
          <p>
            When the first pass and the check run on models from two different labs and the check finishes, the
            summary's footer says <strong>Checked by a second lab</strong>. Vector reads each model's lab from its
            vendor; when it can't tell, the footer says nothing about labs. On Vector Codium, a review without a
            <code>triageModel</code> runs your plan's review pair: the first pass on one lab's model and the check on
            another lab's. If the two would come from the same lab, the review runs one pass and says so.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set <code>panel</code> to true in <code>.vector/review.json</code> for a three-lab panel: once the check
            finishes, a judge from a third lab makes the final call on each finding it looked at, and the footer says
            <strong>Checked by three labs</strong>. The judge is <code>judgeModel</code>, or on Vector Codium your
            plan's panel judge. The panel needs a first pass, a check and a judge from three labs; without them the
            review runs without the judge and says why. The judge works within the same step and time limits as the
            check. Both the panel and the footer line work with your own keys too.
          </p>

          <h3 id="noise-control">Noise control</h3>
          <p>
            Selection is where a review stops being a wall of text. A finding below <code>minConfidence</code> (0.7) or
            below <code>minSeverity</code> (concern) does not go on a line, and anything outside the changed lines is
            listed in the summary instead. Nits and style points are dropped unless <code>nits</code> is true or
            <code>minSeverity</code> is nit. The same defect reported at several places in one file, with the same code or
            the same fix at each, becomes one finding that names the other lines, and a later review doesn't post a
            place again while the comment that names it is open.
          </p>
          <p>
            The cap for a run is the smaller of <code>maxComments</code> (10) and what is left of
            <code>maxCommentsPerPr</code> (25) across the whole pull request; 50 is the ceiling either can be raised
            to. Findings over the cap move to the summary rather than disappearing.
          </p>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            One finding ignores the caps entirely: instructions in the pull request aimed at an AI reviewer. That is
            the finding a maintainer most needs to see, so it is always posted.
          </p>

          <h3 id="between-runs">What carries between runs</h3>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div>
              <dt>Finding identity</dt>
              <dd>
                A finding's id is a fingerprint of its file, its category, the meaningful words of its title and the
                code it points at — never the line number. A force-push or a rebase moves the line without creating a
                duplicate comment.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Status</dt>
              <dd>
                Hidden markers in the comment bodies carry <code>open</code>, <code>fixed</code>,
                <code>dismissed</code> and <code>outdated</code> forward. The author or anyone with write access can
                resolve a comment or react with a thumbs-down, and that finding is not raised again.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Team memory</dt>
              <dd>
                Dismissals are read across the repository, not just this pull request: up to thirty patterns of
                category, directory and title words. It is how the reviewer stops repeating a kind of comment your team
                has already said it does not want.
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>

          <h3 id="posting">Posting</h3>
          <p>
            Vectorscope posts one review per run with the event <strong>comment</strong>. It never approves a pull
            request and never requests changes; the only way it can block a merge is the check you opt into with
            <code>failOn</code>. The summary comment is edited in place rather than repeated, and each push adds only
            comments that are new.
          </p>
          <p>
            If GitHub refuses a batch of inline comments, the batch is split and each half posted on its own; a single
            comment GitHub will not take moves to the summary with its file and line, so no finding is silently lost.
          </p>

          <h3 id="limits">Limits</h3>
          <p>Every run is bounded. Before each step Vectorscope prices the next one and stops if it would cross a line.</p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>maxSteps</dt><dd>30 model steps per specialist.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>maxCostUsd</dt><dd>$2.00 for one review.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>maxCostUsdPerPr</dt><dd>$10.00 across the automatic reviews of one pull request.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>maxCostUsdPerMonth</dt><dd>Off by default; <code>vector github install</code> writes $50 into the workflow for paid models.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>timeoutMinutes</dt><dd>12 minutes.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>maxFiles / maxChangedLines</dt><dd>300 files, 5,000 lines, after the ignores.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            A run that stops early is asked to finalize — return what it has confirmed rather than nothing — so a
            budget or a timeout still produces a review. A review can go past its budget by at most one model step, and
            it says so when it stops early. The monthly cap is best-effort on very busy repositories.
          </p>

          <h3 id="security">Security</h3>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Read-only by construction</strong><span>Permission rules, not a prompt: no edit, no write, no patch, no shell. The local <code>--checks</code> option allows a short list of bare test and typecheck commands, never with flags, because flags on those commands can run any program.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Untrusted text is fenced</strong><span>Everything the pull request controls — code, titles, comments — is wrapped in untrusted tags, and any tag-like text inside it is escaped, so it cannot close its own block or open a trusted one.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Forks never run</strong><span>A fork's review resolves the checkout to the merge base. Its code is read as text and never executed, and automatic reviews of forks are off because GitHub gives those runs no secrets.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Settings come from the base</strong><span><code>.vector/review.md</code> and <code>.vector/review.json</code> are read from the base branch, so a pull request cannot change the rules it is reviewed under.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Secrets are stripped</strong><span>Secret-looking values are redacted from everything posted, and the security reviewer is told to write the same placeholder rather than a value.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Least privilege</strong><span>Reviews post as <code>github-actions[bot]</code> and need only <code>contents: read</code> and <code>pull-requests: write</code>.</span></li>
          </ul>

</DocsPage>
